@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Modeling Failures: Optional → Result → Either → Validation") {
        You'll validate a username through four small iterations. At each
        step the requirements grow just enough that the previous type stops
        being sufficient — and a new one earns its place. By the end you'll
        know exactly which of the four failure types to reach for, and why.
    }

    @Section(title: "Start with Optional") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The simplest possible question: is there a username at all? An
            empty string means "nothing was entered." `Optional<String>`
            models exactly that — no reason is needed yet, just presence or
            absence.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Write a function that treats an empty string as "no username
                was given." `String?` (`Optional<String>`) is enough to
                express that: either the value is there, or it isn't.

                @Code(file: "step01.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Transform the value inside the `Optional` without unwrapping
                it manually. `<£>` (or the named `.map`) applies a function
                only when a value is present, and leaves `nil` untouched.

                @Code(file: "step02.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Collapse the `Optional` to a plain value with `fold`, giving
                a fallback for the `nil` case. This is where `Optional` runs
                out of road: when parsing fails, all you know is that it
                failed. There's no way to say *why* — was the input empty,
                too short, malformed? The next section adds that missing
                piece.

                @Code(file: "step03.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Add error context with Result") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Now the rule set grows: a username must not just be present, it
            must also be at least three characters long. Two different
            things can go wrong, and callers deserve to know which one
            happened. `Result<Success, Failure: Error>` ties a real reason to
            every failure.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Introduce an error type describing everything that can go
                wrong, and rewrite `parseUsername` to return a `Result`
                instead of an `Optional`.

                @Code(file: "step04.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add the second rule as its own small function, also
                returning a `Result`.

                @Code(file: "step05.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Chain the two steps with `>>-` (bind / `flatMap`). If parsing
                fails, `validateLength` never runs — the first failure
                short-circuits the rest of the chain. Unlike `Optional`,
                every failure now carries a concrete `UsernameError`
                explaining what went wrong — that's the whole reason to
                reach for `Result` once "it failed" isn't enough and you need
                "it failed *because*…".

                @Code(file: "step06.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Go symmetric with Either") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `Result` requires its failure type to conform to `Error`. Most of
            the time that's fine, but sometimes the "error" is just a plain
            value — a `String`, an enum with no `Error` conformance, or even
            another meaningful value that isn't a failure at all. `Either`
            expresses the exact same two-way choice as `Result`, without
            that constraint, and without privileging either side.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Drop the `Error` conformance and express the same two rules
                with `Either<UsernameError, String>` instead of `Result`.
                `.left` plays the role `.failure` played; `.right` plays the
                role `.success` played. Everything from the `Result` section
                carries over unchanged — `>>-`, `.flatMap`, the
                short-circuiting behavior — because `Either` and `Result`
                share the same `Functor`/`Monad` shape.

                @Code(file: "step07.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Transform both sides at once with `bimap` — useful when you
                want to present either outcome as a display string.

                @Code(file: "step08.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Collapse an `Either` to a single value with `match`, handling
                both cases explicitly. Reach for `Either` instead of `Result`
                when the failure type isn't (or shouldn't be) an `Error`, or
                when both sides carry equally meaningful information rather
                than one side being a clear "success" and the other a clear
                "failure."

                @Code(file: "step09.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Accumulate every failure with Validation") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The username now has two *independent* rules: it must be at
            least three characters long, and it must not contain spaces. An
            input can violate both at once. `Result` and `Either` only ever
            report the first failure they hit — `Validation` runs every rule
            and reports all of them together.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Reuse the `Result`-based approach from before, adding a
                second rule for spaces. Chain both with `>>-` and watch what
                happens when an input breaks both rules. `" a"` is both too
                short *and* contains a space, but the chain stops at the
                first failure — the caller never learns about the space
                problem. For a form field, that means the user fixes one
                mistake, resubmits, and immediately hits the next one.

                @Code(file: "step10.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Express the same two rules as `Validation<[String], String>`
                instead. The shape is almost identical to `Result` — only the
                failure case now holds a collection of messages.

                @Code(file: "step11.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Combine both validations with `Validation.zip`. Unlike
                `flatMap`, this runs *both* rules regardless of whether the
                first one failed, then merges their errors with
                `Semigroup.combine` — for `[String]`, that's simply
                concatenation. Both problems are reported in a single pass —
                the exact outcome `Result`'s short-circuiting chain couldn't
                produce.

                @Code(file: "step12.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                When only one rule fails, only that one error appears; when
                both succeed, `zip` collects both values into a tuple. This
                is why `Validation` has no `flatMap`: accumulating every
                error requires running all rules *independently*, which is
                incompatible with `flatMap`'s inherently sequential,
                short-circuiting nature. `Validation` is an `Applicative`,
                not a `Monad` — and that trade-off is exactly what makes it
                the right tool once "report everything wrong at once"
                becomes a requirement.

                @Code(file: "step13.swift", name: "ParseUsername.swift")
            }
        }
    }
}
